        "video_script": "Number six: the counterfeit self. Researchers gave women real designer sunglasses, a brand that sells for hundreds of dollars. But some were told their pair came from a box labelled counterfeit. Others, from a box labelled authentic. In reality, every pair was genuine. Then the women wore the sunglasses while they solved maths puzzles, and graded their own work, with a chance to cheat for more money. Among women who thought their sunglasses were authentic, about thirty percent cheated. Among those who thought they were wearing fakes: seventy-one percent. The same glasses. Only the label in their head had changed. And it wasn't a fluke: with a new group, randomly assigned, the gap was almost the same. Sixty-nine percent against twenty-six. In follow-up studies, wearing the 'fakes' also made people more suspicious of others. The researchers' explanation: when you wear something that sends a false signal, part of you feels like a fake. And once you feel a bit fake, the next dishonest step feels smaller. Pretending to be richer than you are doesn't just risk your wallet. It can quietly change how you see yourself. Try this today: choose things that are honestly yours, even if they're simpler. Feeling genuine is worth more than looking expensive. And now, the question that shows how strange our relationship with money really is.",
